# MicroJS8 — DigiRig USB serial port stable name
# =====================================================================
#
# The DigiRig Mobile uses a Silicon Labs CP2102 USB-to-UART bridge
# (vendor 10c4:ea60). This is the SAME chip used by many other USB
# serial adapters, so we match by vendor:product but with an extra
# serial-number filter where possible — DigiRig devices have a
# unique serial number programmed at the factory.
#
# What this rule creates:
#   /dev/digirig    →  symlink to whichever ttyUSBx the kernel
#                      assigned to the CP2102
#
# Why we need it:
#   The launcher script (microjs8-rigctld-launcher) and the daemon's
#   RtsPttService both expect /dev/digirig to be the canonical
#   stable name. Without this rule, the kernel-assigned ttyUSBx
#   number depends on enumeration order — could be 0 or 1 depending
#   on whether other USB serial devices came up first.
#
# Group membership:
#   GROUP="dialout" gives the microjs8 user (which is in the dialout
#   group) read/write access without world-writable mode bits.
#
# Adding more DigiRig units:
#   If you have multiple DigiRigs and need to disambiguate, add a
#   second rule line that matches ATTRS{serial}=="<unique-serial>"
#   and creates /dev/digirig1, /dev/digirig2, etc. Then add a new
#   RadioDef in radios.py with preferred_serial_path="/dev/digirig1".
#
# Verifying:
#   sudo udevadm trigger
#   ls -l /dev/digirig
#   udevadm info /dev/ttyUSB0  # see all the ATTRS{} for matching

# Match: USB serial adapter, Silicon Labs CP2102, on a tty interface.
# The CP2102 enumerates as a single CDC-ACM-like interface; no
# ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM filter needed (vs the QDX which has two).
#
# ENV{ID_GPSD_IGNORE}="1" tells gpsd's hotplug script to leave this
# device alone — gpsd's default udev rule (60-gpsd.rules in the
# gpsd package) would otherwise grab any USB serial device looking
# for NMEA. On a CP210x this is catastrophic: gpsd opens with RTS
# asserted high, the optoisolator keys the radio's PTT, and the
# CP210x chip latches RTS high until a USB device reset. We don't
# want gpsd touching the Digirig under any circumstances — gpsd
# should only ever talk to actual GPS receivers (the u-blox CDC
# ACM on /dev/ttyACM0).
#
# ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1" does the same for ModemManager,
# which also probes USB serial devices for AT command responses.
# Same failure mode as gpsd: probing involves opening the port
# which asserts RTS on CP210x.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", \
    SYMLINK+="digirig", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", \
    ENV{ID_GPSD_IGNORE}="1", ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1"
